1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,684
Hi. Today we're going to be looking at animals in urban environments

2
00:00:06,684 --> 00:00:14,800
and I'm going to be telling you about some research on how they're affected by these environments.

3
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:19,900
Now, in evolutionary terms, urban environments represent huge upheavals,

4
00:00:19,900 --> 00:00:26,700
the sorts of massive changes that usually happen over millions of years.

5
00:00:26,700 --> 00:00:32,800
And we used to think that only a few species could adapt to this new environment.

6
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:38,650
One species which is well known as being highly adaptable is the crow,

7
00:00:38,650 --> 00:00:44,500
and there've been various studies about how they manage to learn new skills.

8
00:00:44,500 --> 00:00:46,941
Another successful species is the pigeon,

9
00:00:46,941 --> 00:00:52,231
because they're able to perch on ledges on the walls of city buildings,

10
00:00:52,231 --> 00:00:56,300
just like they once perched on cliffs by the sea.

11
00:00:56,300 --> 00:01:05,859
But in fact, we're now finding that these early immigrants were just the start of a more general movement of animals into cities,

12
00:01:05,859 --> 00:01:09,600
and of adaptation by these animals to city life.

13
00:01:09,600 --> 00:01:16,055
And one thing that researchers are finding especially interesting is the speed with

14
00:01:16,055 --> 00:01:20,140
which they're doing this - we're not talking about gradual evolution here

15
00:01:20,440 --> 00:01:24,000
these animals are changing fast.

16
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:29,200
Let me tell you about some of the studies that have been carried out in this area.

17
00:01:29,200 --> 00:01:34,535
So, in the University of Minnesota, a biologist called Emilie Snell-Rood

18
00:01:34,535 --> 00:01:40,840
and her colleagues looked at specimens of urbanised small mammals such as mice

19
00:01:40,840 --> 00:01:44,840
and gophers that had been collected in Minnesota

20
00:01:45,420 --> 00:01:48,600
and that are now kept in museums there.

21
00:01:48,600 --> 00:01:54,078
And she looked at specimens that had been collected over the last hundred years,

22
00:01:54,078 --> 00:01:57,600
which is a very short time in evolutionary terms.

23
00:01:57,600 --> 00:02:00,559
And she found that during that time,

24
00:02:00,559 --> 00:02:03,860
these small mammals had experienced a jump in brain size

25
00:02:03,860 --> 00:02:06,900
when compared to rural mammals.

26
00:02:06,900 --> 00:02:11,061
Now, we can't be sure this means they're more intelligent,

27
00:02:11,061 --> 00:02:16,055
but since the sizes of other parts of the body didn't change,

28
00:02:16,055 --> 00:02:19,800
it does suggest that something cognitive was going on.

29
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:26,378
And Snell-Rood thinks that this change might reflect the cognitive demands of adjusting to city life -

30
00:02:26,378 --> 00:02:29,880
having to look in different places to find food

31
00:02:29,900 --> 00:02:34,600
for example, and coping with a whole new set of dangers.

32
00:02:34,600 --> 00:02:37,980
Then over in Germany at the Max Planck Institute

33
00:02:38,100 --> 00:02:42,156
there's another biologist called Catarina Miranda

34
00:02:42,156 --> 00:02:48,200
who's done some experiments with blackbirds living in urban and rural areas.

35
00:02:48,200 --> 00:02:53,900
And she's been looking not at their anatomy but at their behaviour.

36
00:02:53,900 --> 00:02:55,580
So as you might expect

37
00:02:56,020 --> 00:02:59,864
she's found that the urban blackbirds tend to be quite bold -

38
00:02:59,864 --> 00:03:06,200
they're prepared to face up to a lot of threats that would frighten away their country counterparts.

39
00:03:06,200 --> 00:03:12,957
But there's one type of situation that does seem to frighten the urban blackbirds,

40
00:03:12,957 --> 00:03:17,300
and that's anything new - anything they haven't experienced before.

41
00:03:17,300 --> 00:03:22,500
And if you think about it, that's quite sensible for a bird living in the city.

42
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:25,720
Jonathan Atwell, in Indiana University

43
00:03:26,060 --> 00:03:30,800
is looking at how a range of animals respond to urban environments.

44
00:03:30,800 --> 00:03:34,041
He's found that when they're under stress,

45
00:03:34,041 --> 00:03:43,300
their endocrine systems react by reducing the amount of hormones such as corticosterone into their blood. It's a sensible-seeming adaptation.

46
00:03:43,300 --> 00:03:50,300
A rat that gets scared every time a subway train rolls past won't be very successful.

47
00:03:50,300 --> 00:03:52,920
There's just one more study I'd like to mention

48
00:03:53,080 --> 00:03:57,248
which is by Sarah Partan and her team,

49
00:03:57,248 --> 00:04:02,152
and they've been looking at how squirrels communicate in an urban environment,

50
00:04:02,152 --> 00:04:09,100
and they've found that a routine part of their communication is carried out by waving their tails.

51
00:04:09,100 --> 00:04:12,474
You do also see this in the country,

52
00:04:12,474 --> 00:04:14,200
but it's much more prevalent in cities

53
00:04:14,700 --> 00:04:18,800
possibly because it's effective in a noisy environment.

54
00:04:18,800 --> 00:04:23,067
So what are the long-term implications of this?

55
00:04:23,067 --> 00:04:30,000
One possibility is that we may see completely new species developing in cities.

56
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:31,260
But on the other hand

57
00:04:31,780 --> 00:04:36,800
it's possible that not all of these adaptations will be permanent.

58
00:04:36,800 --> 00:04:40,080
Once the animal's got accustomed to its new environment

59
00:04:40,700 --> 00:04:44,300
it may no longer need the features it's developed.

60
00:04:44,300 --> 00:04:49,000
So, now we've had a look...
